--- body ---
## Context

Stages 2 and 5 of the pipeline in [[1500]]. Today each extractor is bespoke and
writes its own lines (`build_idn_cipp_priority_projects.py`,
`build_sen_plan_projects.py`, `_vnm_inventory.py`, ...). The core gives them one
way to read a document and one way to write lines, without rewriting the
layout-specific parsers, which are research code and stay as plug-ins.

## Work

- Registry interface: given a `document_id`, return its metadata and the
  bytes of its snapshots by `sha256`. Today it reads `documents.csv`,
  `retrievals.csv` and `data/jetp/documents/objects/`; a later export from a
  document library must swap in without touching extractors.
- Writer: append-only lines under `lines.d/` with `run_id`, method, method
  version, `status` and `adapter_version`; `line-field-specs` rows; minted
  `line_id`s that continue ordinals across snapshots (extraction § 9), never
  renumbered; a `runs` row per run. Tables and columns: [[1870]].
- Dispositions: every registered document, and every extracted snapshot,
  ends with lines or a row of the `dispositions` journal of one of the eight
  kinds of `docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 7, with its reason, not silence.
- Idempotence: re-running on an already extracted snapshot changes nothing.
- Dated snapshots (author, 2026-09-30: "append dated lines. For old and new
  contents: republishing old is a (weak) confirmation that it still stands
  true"). A living document ([[1184]]) or a new version of a document gets a
  new snapshot. Extracting it appends lines tied to that snapshot's `sha256`
  and retrieval date; the lines of earlier snapshots never change. A line
  whose content reappears unchanged in the later snapshot is kept for that
  snapshot too, not dropped as a duplicate: it dates a restatement, which is
  weak evidence that the earlier statement still held on the later date. So
  the ledger answers, for any stated content, every snapshot date that stated
  it. As accepted in spec v1 (extraction § 8): identical bytes or identical
  normalised text extract nothing and record a whole-snapshot restatement;
  a restated line is linked by a `restatements` row to its origin, never to
  a previous restatement; pairing is key-based at M2; an unpaired earlier
  item is recorded, never read as a cancellation. This is what makes the pipeline the extraction step of the periodic
  harvest (M4), not only a one-off backlog run.
- Parser changes (carried from the deleted backend design, § 6): a parser
  change that moves a locator keeps the existing `line_id` through a reviewed
  crosswalk when the meaning is unchanged, and otherwise corrects the line as
  a ledger error. Neither a mutable locator nor a content hash alone defines a
  line's identity.
- Pending list: one command lists the snapshots that have neither lines nor a
  disposition. It is the input of every later run, backlog or periodic.
- Build from the first real case (the VNM progress-update series) and
  generalise after two or three cases.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One entry point runs a named document through registry, extractor and writer
- [ ] Re-running on an extracted snapshot leaves the tree byte-identical (test)
- [ ] A second dated snapshot yields lines tied to that snapshot, with the lines of the earlier snapshot byte-identical; a value restated unchanged is a restatement linked to its origin; tested on a fixture with one value changed and on the held page whose bytes change on every request (extraction § 13), since no held document has a genuine second version
- [ ] One command lists the snapshots with neither lines nor a disposition (test)
- [ ] A missing snapshot, a duplicate and an empty document each yield a `dispositions` row of the right kind and grain (tests)
- [ ] Every run writes a `runs` row and every line it writes names that `run_id` (test)
- [ ] A defect replayed against the writer is rejected (red test): a renumbered `line_id`, a header that disagrees with `line-field-specs`
- [ ] `make jetp-ledger-check`, `make check-fast`, `make lint` and `make check-domain-jetp` pass
